  "strategy": "**Approach and rationale**\n\nWe approach this challenge by leveraging multi-phase optimization with extended adaptive phases and a refined gradient scheme. Unlike the previous program which used local search in the simplex space, we adopt a hierarchical selection method based on periodicity. We assume the optimal sequence shows cyclic patterns with particular rational frequencies, and we use harmonic analysis to guide our search. \n\nThe key intuition is that good sequences often have rotational symmetry or self-similar patterns. Therefore, we aim to minimize the maximum over normalized sequences, including the $n=448$ sine initialization. We balance exploration and exploitation by systematically sweeping through different harmonic frequency profiles.\n\n**Mathematical specification**\n\nGiven a sequence length $n$, we define our optimization target:\n$$\n\\min_{\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n_+} \\frac{2n\\cdot \\max(\\mathbf{a} * \\mathbf{a}))}{(\\sum \\mathbf{a})^2}\n$$\n\nOur solution strategy is:\n\n1. Initialization: \n   - If `$height_sequence_1` provides a valid base: project it to have sum=1\n   - Else: use a normalized symmetric sine wave approximation\n\n2.Adaptive harmonic phase:\n   We maintain sequences with explicit harmonic structure across multiple frequencies ($f$). The convolution process reveals key frequencies shaping the solution.\n\n3.Surface perception:\n   Our core search strategy will:\n\n   - Start from given parent at `$height_sequence_1`\n   - Generate candidate sequences by convolving base patterns\n   - Use a two-heuristic\u8005\u304c\u8868 (2-phase search) method to alternate between ascent and descent\n\n   The surface skip strategy (SSS) uses:\n   - Forward gradient ascent for escaping lower dimensions\n   - Backward gradient descent for converging towards optimum\n   - periodic resampling of coefficients\n\n**Implementation procedure**\n\nWe implement the optimization kernel as follows:\n\n1.Auxiliary functions:\n\n   def smooth_normalization(v, tol=1e-7):\n       s = np.sum(v)\n       if s < tol:\n           raise ValueError(\"Invalid sequence\")\n       # Clipping to [0,10000] per security checks\n       v = np.clip(v, 0, 10000)\n       return v / s\n\n   def gaussian_window(length, sigma):\n       \"\"\"Returns [0, length-1] with multi-modal peaks\"\"\"\n       x = np.linspace(-length/2, length/2, length)\n       norm = 1/(np.sqrt(2*np.pi)*sigma)\n       return norm * np.exp(-(x**2)/(2*sigma**2))\n\n   def directional_step(seq, alpha, beta):\n       \"\"\"Applies decay factor updates to simplex projected sequence\"\"\"\n       n = len(seq)\n       # Using coefdy management\n       idx1, idx2 = np.random.randint(0, n-1, size=2)\n       new_seq = alpha*smooth_normalization(seq)**2 + beta*gaussian_window(n,0.7)[idx1:idx2]\n       new_seq /= np.sum(new_seq)\n       return new_seq\n\n2.Phase 1: Ramp-up with centralized uniform vectors\n\n   ```python\n   def multi_ramp_phase(n_central):\n       timeout = 5  # seconds needed for initialization\n       for n_size in [32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1024]:\n           temp = ttk.default_rng(42)\n           s = temp.random(2*n_size-1)\n           s = smooth_normalization(s)\n           score_initial = evaluate_sequence(s.tolist())\n           progress()  # Small progress\n           # Get better solutions in simplex projection\n           s = optimize_simp(s.tolist(), tolerance=1e-8)\n           score_final = evaluate_sequence(s.tolist())\n           if score_final < target_threshold:\n               return s, n_size\n       return uniform_init(n_central), n_central\n   ```\n\n3.Phase 2: Centralized directed bin-search with exponential gradients\n\n   ```python\n   def directed_bin_search(initial, time_budget):\n       t = 0\n       # Create binary decision tree-like structure\n       while t < time_budget*0.8 and not optimized:\n           # Skip any invalid moves\n           new_seq = smooth_transition(initial, rate=0.90)\n           if np.isinf(evaluate_sequence(new_seq.tolist())): continue\n           comparisons = compute_all_conv(new_seq)\n           win_cond = argsort_comparisons(comparisons)\n           peaks = win_cond.back_top\n           # Project towards peaks\n           peak_vector = smooth_normalization(peaks)\n           # Apply Frank-Wolfe algorithm\n           new_peak = (1-alpha)*initial + alpha*peak_vector\n           score_next = evaluate_sequence(new_peak.tolist())\n           if score_next <= best_score - 0.005:\n               initial = new_peak\n           else:\n               initial -= 0.5*peak_vector  # Make adversarial shift\n           t += 0.1*60  # Assume 10 seconds per step\n       return build_best_sequence(initial, early_stop_at=10)\n   ```\n\n4.Phase 3: Distributed harmonic style search\n\n   ```python\n   def harmonic_cycle_search(initial_length, max_time):\n       n_sequence = initial_length if initial_length>=32 else 512\n       freq_grid = [0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4]  # Frequencies to consider\n       best_seq = uniform_init(n_sequence)\n       best_score = evaluate_sequence(best_seq.tolist())\n       t_end = time.time() + max_time\n       for freq in freq_grid:\n           if time.time() >= t_end: break\n           base_freq = freq_values_prior\n           x = np.arange(n_sequence)\n           sin_wave = np.sin(2*PI*base_freq*x)/10  # Very small amplitude \n           nonnegative = np.clip(sin_wave, 0, None)\n           normalized = nonnegative/np.sum(non_oajective(val))\n           score = evaluate_sequence(normalized.tolist())\n           if score < best_score:\n               best_seq = normalized\n               best_score = score\n       # Repeat frequency sweeps\n       return harmonic_mix(best_seq)\n   ```\n\n5.Phase 4: Adaptive short-wave oscillation handling with central unit handling\n\n   ```python\n   def oscillatory_vector_optimization(initial, time_budget):\n       t_start = time.time()\n       current = initial\n       history = [evaluate_sequence(initial.tolist()), None]\n       for f in range(10, 105, 10):\n           if time.time()-t_start > time_budget*0.8: break\n           # Apply short-wave perturbation (very high frequency)\n           pert = gaussian_window(4, loc=4)  # Example that acts as localized spike\n           new = (1-0.1)*current + 0.1*pert\n           score = evaluate_sequence(new.tolist())\n           if score < history[0]:\n               history[0], history[1] = score, new\n           # Use binary search over [0,1] scaling factors\n           for alpha in [0.1, 0.2, ..., 1.0]:\n               temp = (1-alpha)/current + alpha*pert\n               if np.all(temp>=0) and np.sum(temp)>=0.01:\n                   score_temp = evaluate_sequence(temp.tolist())\n                   if score_temp < history[0]:\n                       history[0], history[1] = score_temp, temp\n       return history[1]  # The optimized sequence\n   ```\n\n6.Ultimate termination check:\n\n   ```python\n   def propose_candidate(parent=None, time_limit=1800, seed=42):\n       \"\"\"Perform up to 1800 seconds of backend adapter work\"\"\"\n       if parent is not None and sum(parent)>=0.01:\n           seq0 = parent\n       else:\n           seq0 = uniform_init(448, scale=2)\n       best_seq = seq0\n       best_score = evaluate_sequence(best.seq.tolist())\n\n       current_time = time.time()\n       time_left = time_limit - (current_time - start_time)\n\n       # Select various frequency profiles to explore\n       freq_profiles = [\n           \"high_frequency_flat\",\n           \"harmonic_mixture\",\n           \"variable_frequency_density\"\n       ]\n\n       for profile in freq_profiles:\n           if time_left < 30: break\n           if not time_available(profile, time_left): continue\n           cand = generate_freq_profile(seed+profile.index, profile_param=seed+1)\n           score = evaluate_sequence(cand.tolist())\n           if score < best_score:\n               best_seq = cand\n               best_score = score\n               time_left *= 0.8  # Reduced time budget\n\n       # Cleanse best candidate with simplex projection \n       best_seq = smooth_normalization(best_seq)\n       return best_seq.tolist()\n\n   ```\n\n**Search choices and useful variations**\n\n1.Initialization varies between phases: \n\n   - If `$height_sequence_1` is provided and valid, use it base\n   - Else, use very defined sine-shapes at default 448\n\n2.Profile variants:\n\n   a.\"Low_freq_clockwork\": minimize the higher frequency components by gradually increasing frequency resolution.\n      Helps if we are stuck at lower dimensions.\n\n   b.\"High_freq_scramble\": force maximum disturbances to break local minima, then fallback to stable peaks.\n      Useful if optimization has plateaued.\n\n   c.\"Mixed_frequency_convolution\": enforce both positive and negative states by Balancing distribution of factors around convolution.\n      Works for very large $n$.\n\n**Compute budget and robustness**\n\nThe program organizes into phases:\n\n- Phase 1: Multi-length winner adaptation (200 seconds)\n- Phase 2: Full-length simplex projection (600 seconds)\n- Phase 3: Atomic refinements under harmonic oscillation (1000 seconds)\n- Overall: 1800 seconds maximum.\n\nDistributed optimization: we never finish optimization before returning. We schedule evaluations carefully.\n\n**Validation and return contract**\n\nThe program ensures all evaluations are valid and minimized objective score. We compare using `evaluate_sequence` at every significant candidate gereration.\n\nKey outputs:\n\n- `harmonic_cycle_search`: harmonic oscillation-based projects\n- `smooth_normalization`: use for secure return fits\n- `best_seq_obj`: maintains validity and promotion\n\nThe code is structured to be direct-callable for simplex search. All operations are clearly documented.",
